<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djenné</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Djenné is a city in the inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, famous for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> Great Mosque. A major centre of trade and Islamic learning, it linked the forest zone to the trans-Saharan network. It is associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and was a key commercial city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djenne.jpg" alt="Djenné" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: qiv · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moschee_von_Djenn%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Djenné sits on the floodplain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Bani rivers at the southern end of the Inland Niger Delta, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. The annual flood begins in July and reaches its maximum in October, and the town then becomes an island reached by causeways. Some 2.5 kilometres to the south-east lies the archaeological site of Djenné-Djeno, one of the earliest and most important urban sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, first settled around 200 BCE according to excavations by Susan and Roderick McIntosh. The present town was first settled after 1000 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Djenné-Djeno developed into a large walled urban complex between 300 and 850 CE. After 1100 its population declined, and by 1400 it was abandoned, for reasons that are unknown. Djenné itself survived as a centre of regional trade. Between the 15th and 17th centuries much of the trans-Saharan trade in salt, gold and slaves passing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> went through it, and both towns were centres of Islamic scholarship. Seventeenth-century chronicles give conflicting accounts of its relation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. Sonni Ali conquered it in 1471 after a siege of about six months, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> it had about 40,000 people.</p><p>A Moroccan army defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Tondibi</a> in 1591, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>'s attempt to fill the void was beaten outside Djenné's walls in 1599. The town formed part of the Segou kingdom from 1670 to 1818 and the Massina Empire from 1818 to 1861. René Caillié, in 1828, was the first European to visit it. French forces occupied it in April 1893 and made Mopti the regional capital, so that Djenné's importance declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Djenné is a town and urban commune of the Mopti Region. It is noted for its Sudanese-style architecture, in which nearly all buildings, among them the Great Mosque, are of sun-baked earthen bricks coated with plaster, and the adobe needs replastering at least every other year. The town is a centre of Islamic scholarship, with Quranic schools that draw students from outside the region. In 1988 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named the Old Towns of Djenné, comprising four archaeological sites, a World Heritage Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Djenné” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1754 he defeated the rival Massassi rulers of Kaarta to the north-west and captured their king, Foulakoro, leaving Ségou the dominant power between Bamako and <b>Djenné</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cities such as Timbuktu, <b>Djenné</b> and Gao became centres of trade, Islamic scholarship and the arts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His second was the trading city of <b>Djenné</b> in the inland delta, which, according to tradition, resisted the Siege of Djenné for seven years before submitting around 1473.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Timbuktu and <b>Djenné</b> grew into centres of learning, and before Mali lost Timbuktu in 1433 a jurist from the Hejaz found the city full of West African scholars who knew more law than he did.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li></ul></section>
